Ask the marketplace for the alpine build on Android.

Extensions that ship native code are published per target platform, and VS Code
picks one from isAlpineLinux() plus the arch. On Android that resolves to
linux-arm64, which is the glibc build -- and a glibc binary cannot start in an
app process. glibc's __tls_init_tp calls set_robust_list and rseq during startup,
Android's app seccomp filter rejects both, and the process dies with SIGSYS
before main(). The rseq tunable does not help, because set_robust_list is still
called and has no tunable.

The musl build makes neither call and runs. Measured with the Claude Code CLI on
an API 36 emulator: the linux-arm64 binary dies with SIGSYS, the alpine-arm64 one
prints its version -- loaded by musl's ld.so out of the extension directory,
since SELinux also refuses execve() of anything under filesDir for targetSdk >=
29 while still granting the map and execute a loader needs.

isAlpineLinux() cannot work this out for itself: it reads /etc/os-release, and
Android neither ships that file nor allows creating it, so it answers false.

The side effect is global: this decides the target platform for every extension,
not just this one. Checked against Open VSX before landing, because the fear is
reasonable -- a musl .node dlopen'd into our Bionic Node would be strictly worse
than a glibc one, which the compatibility shim can load when its imports are
ones the build found in the addons it scanned.

Of 18 extensions probed, only four publish per-platform builds at all:
Anthropic.claude-code, charliermarsh.ruff, rust-lang.rust-analyzer and
redhat.java. The last two also publish a genuine universal artifact, and
isTargetPlatformCompatible() in extensionManagement.ts returns true for UNIVERSAL
whatever the product platform is, so they are untouched. ruff does publish an
alpine build, and both of its variants contain zero .node files -- its payload is
a standalone Rust executable run as a subprocess, the same shape as Claude Code.
So no extension in that sample hands a musl .node to our loader.

For that standalone-executable shape alpine is not merely harmless but better: a
glibc executable can never start here at all, while a musl one runs through the
loader.

Two residual risks worth naming rather than pretending away. An extension
publishing per-platform builds *including* alpine whose payload is a dlopen'd
.node would regress -- none was found, and it would be an odd thing to publish,
since an alpine variant of a Node addon targets Alpine containers. And an
extension publishing per-platform builds with neither an alpine variant nor a
universal fallback becomes uninstallable rather than merely broken; the symptom
is the marketplace reporting no version available for this platform.

The debug line is not decoration: the choice is otherwise invisible, and it is
also the only text in this hunk that survives minification, so it is what the
Verify stage greps for.

